WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.660
Yeast cells mate with three kinases in a row.

2
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:13.960
Ste11 switches on Ste7, and Ste7 switches on Fus3.

3
00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:21.220
But Ste11 also starts the cascade for too much salt, and the one for growing in long

4
00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:24.960
filaments. One enzyme, three destinations.

5
00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:31.633
Elaine Elion's laboratory was working out which parts of a yeast protein three

6
00:00:31.633 --> 00:00:35.373
enzymes stuck to. The protein was Ste5.

7
00:00:36.373 --> 00:00:38.513
Without it, a yeast cell cannot mate.

8
00:00:39.553 --> 00:00:42.173
Yet Ste5 had no enzyme activity.

9
00:00:42.733 --> 00:00:45.773
It never put a phosphate on anything, or took one off.

10
00:00:46.873 --> 00:00:50.893
In 1994, that came close to saying it had no mechanism.

11
00:00:51.833 --> 00:01:00.673
Choi, Satterberg, Lyons and Elion reported that Ste11, Ste7, and Fus3 bind

12
00:01:00.673 --> 00:01:07.213
different regions of Ste5, so one Ste5 molecule can hold all three at once.

13
00:01:08.173 --> 00:01:12.533
Two other laboratories reached the same result that year, Michael Wigler's and

14
00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:13.533
George Sprague's.

15
00:01:14.117 --> 00:01:16.157
A scaffold buys speed.

16
00:01:16.957 --> 00:01:21.337
A kinase and its target normally find each other by drifting at random.

17
00:01:22.197 --> 00:01:24.637
On one scaffold they are already touching.

18
00:01:25.597 --> 00:01:27.077
It also buys insulation.

19
00:01:28.057 --> 00:01:34.277
The Ste11 that a pheromone switches on sits against Fus3, nowhere near the

20
00:01:34.277 --> 00:01:36.537
kinase that would tell the cell it was drying out.

21
00:01:37.417 --> 00:01:42.357
A protein that catalyzes nothing earns its place by what it buys the cell.
